#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3343ce15-2b39-5a59-898f-f05b1fdaeb6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385716} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5ce95a-3f4c-5eb9-9490-0ac3522a9694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385717} {:answer/id #uuid "8918ebe5-9502-588a-bd62-57f724661f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385718} {:answer/id #uuid "5376295b-5296-5360-80bb-5d5f8c66a884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385719} {:answer/id #uuid "f35917c2-0e8f-5c18-8845-f0681a0bc95e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f35917c2-0e8f-5c18-8845-f0681a0bc95e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385720} :answer-field/id #uuid "73a1b983-2a14-5bd5-8891-d0695f44a963" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d602d4dd-c1db-593a-a69d-b6391433b00a" :question/math-academy-id "q-33063" :question/problem "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ at the point $P(2\\pi ,1,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,j+y(t)\\,j+z(t)\\,k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by \n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2\\pi ,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\pi \\,i+\\,j+0\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k & =2\\pi \\,i+\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=2\\pi  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t=1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=2\\pi .$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(2\\pi ,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(2\\pi ).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2\\pi :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(2\\pi ) & =\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & =i+0\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =i+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(2\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(2\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+0+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(2\\pi )$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2\\pi ,1,0):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2\\pi ) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(2\\pi )}{‖{r}^{′}(2\\pi )‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot (i+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad3c3225-936e-5ddb-8740-1383464fc3d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\,i+\\frac{8}{9}\\,j+\\frac{4}{9}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385723} {:answer/id #uuid "cb19ffd7-3b5e-51ac-a670-7cbc18bae9bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385724} {:answer/id #uuid "e98b6323-9699-590b-942e-de3e219951b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{21}\\,i+\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{21}\\,j+\\frac{4\\sqrt{21}}{21}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385725} {:answer/id #uuid "c163d914-4c41-5823-9018-0b71ad52a136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\,i+\\frac{4}{9}\\,j+\\frac{8}{9}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385726} {:answer/id #uuid "93abbcdd-dda5-5be5-9ec0-2ddc58c78c3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{69}}{69}\\,i+\\frac{2\\sqrt{69}}{69}\\,j+\\frac{8\\sqrt{69}}{69}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c163d914-4c41-5823-9018-0b71ad52a136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\,i+\\frac{4}{9}\\,j+\\frac{8}{9}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385726} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c0ccf0a-27ac-53db-808d-ef3b6a1afb80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8caefcc-50a5-53a2-a269-288ed0a3e61d" :question/math-academy-id "q-33130" :question/problem "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\,k$ at the point $P(2,4,8)?$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,j+y(t)\\,j+z(t)\\,k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,4,8)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\,k & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}=4 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}=8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=2.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(2,4,8)$ is at the tip of $r(2).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+4t\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(2) & =\\,i+2\\cdot 2\\,j+4\\cdot 2\\,k \\\\  & =i+4\\,j+8\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(2)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2,4,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(2)}{‖{r}^{′}(2)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (i+4\\,j+8\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\,i+\\frac{4}{9}\\,j+\\frac{8}{9}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1d5bb41-39a5-5021-9fcf-ec76b5a441e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386088} {:answer/id #uuid "11b36f08-4bb8-5cfb-9bd8-7c889f1be15d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386089} {:answer/id #uuid "a1306070-27b8-54f8-999c-180f53d0f08e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386090} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0d0109-8947-568a-8e09-9c03bddda6e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386091} {:answer/id #uuid "b55dd1b5-bd81-5a92-b705-9806ff8f75f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{2}{5},\\,\\frac{2}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1306070-27b8-54f8-999c-180f53d0f08e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386090} :answer-field/id #uuid "19921ccc-8653-514b-b4e2-8903d06eeea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e40fe88-bf16-5d89-9381-2099d0c7a194" :question/math-academy-id "q-33135" :question/problem "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨4t,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ at the point $P(4,0,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(4,0,1)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨4,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩ & =⟨4,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4t=4 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t=0 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(4,0,1)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{4}{t},\\,2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{4}{(1)},\\,2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,4,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(4,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨4,\\,4,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34342124-8cee-5b70-bf57-64f746af8912" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unit Tangent Vectors Using the Rules of Differentiation For Vector Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "967a8c8e-23f7-59fe-a75f-f3ca150363f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(i-j-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386095} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b42822-77c5-5680-97a0-1257500c7016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386096} {:answer/id #uuid "a408cbef-5dc0-5290-9894-93231f1d0a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(i+j-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386097} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa8dbc6-3a5d-5b64-ae61-3d614f775b9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}(i+j-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386098} {:answer/id #uuid "752ae9eb-bc96-50bb-a880-814a3bf59f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(i+j-2k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7b42822-77c5-5680-97a0-1257500c7016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386096} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1a95f04-b672-52a3-831c-dc923df14119" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c9414af-cb9d-5103-8118-a585c41e78a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-64492" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)={t}^{2}p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=1$, given that  $p(1)=i-\\,j-k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(1)=-i+\\,j+\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\cdot p(t)+{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =2t\\,p(t)+{t}^{2}\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =2(1)\\,p(1)+(1)^{2}\\,{p}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =2(i-\\,j-k)+(-i+\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =2i-2\\,j-2k-i+j+\\,k \\\\  & =i-j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (i-j-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=1$ is  $T(1)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34342124-8cee-5b70-bf57-64f746af8912" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unit Tangent Vectors Using the Rules of Differentiation For Vector Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ff5b82c-6a31-5bea-8e21-b94c774ee623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(2\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385737} {:answer/id #uuid "2d7eb5b0-3b23-50f0-bd08-4b328cc3726e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}\\,i+\\frac{1}{9}\\,j+\\frac{2}{9}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385738} {:answer/id #uuid "d30f135f-7a76-5047-8108-8f0ae2113c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(-2\\,i+\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385739} {:answer/id #uuid "7c24304d-0dfa-5f28-b057-ef4898866955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385740} {:answer/id #uuid "c20a9918-d679-53b5-be45-663273d3009f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c20a9918-d679-53b5-be45-663273d3009f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385741} :answer-field/id #uuid "0969ac4b-bcfa-5c68-8c78-e3e4deff1649" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0205390e-d98e-5033-a317-b12907a8ca1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-75228" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-2t)p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=1$, given that  $p(1)=i+2\\,j-k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(1)=2\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(({t}^{2}-2t)p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot p(t)+({t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =(2t-2)\\,p(t)+({t}^{2}-2t)\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =(2(1)-2)\\,p(1)+((1)^{2}-2(1))\\,{p}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =0-{p}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =-(2\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =-2i+\\,j+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-2\\,i+j+2k) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=1$ is  $T(1)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k.$"}]}
